putTokens":1093,"cachedTokens":0,"outputTokens":5423,"reasoningTokens":4729,"usd":0.024414},{"model":"muse-spark-1.3","purpose":"referee the fits","ms":6837,"inputTokens":1776,"cachedTokens":0,"outputTokens":1427,"reasoningTokens":1237,"usd":0.00828475},{"model":"muse-spark-1.3","purpose":"eyeball baseline (240 frames at 30.0 fps)","ms":11485,"inputTokens":14972,"cachedTokens":9,"outputTokens":1456,"reasoningTokens":1307,"usd":0.024893099999999998}],"usd":0.1403346,"tokens":45006,"ms":78289},"models":{"observe":"muse-spark-1.3","hypothesise":"muse-spark-1.3","critique":"muse-spark-1.3","revise":"muse-wonder-1.0","eyeball":"muse-spark-1.3"},"extras":{"robustness":{"note":"Every way of reading the same footage, each fitted with a plain ballistic law on the airborne frames only (feet off the ground). Error bars include the ±5% ruler. The body box and the mask centroid move with Young’s crouch and arm swing, so the headline follows the helmet top, which is rigid and airborne the whole time; both jumps agree on it to 0.01. Read the spread, 1.64 to 2.12, as a further ±0.2 that depends on what you call the jumper’s position. The broadcast repeats one film frame in five (24 fps film shown at 30 fps), so the helmet stands still on every fifth frame; dropping those 7 frames and refitting moves g from 1.72 to 1.75, inside the error bar.","rows":[{"label":"Jump 1, following the top of the helmet","g":1.7130948695518593,"sigma":0.09156511704869913},{"label":"Jump 1, following the middle of the body box","g":2.115926094611902,"sigma":0.19197750703672228},{"label":"Jump 1, following the centroid of the mask","g":2.0935816676529506,"sigma":0.24335172638930797},{"label":"Jump 2, following the top of the helmet","g":1.7145917137277003,"sigma":0.09025267866770112},{"label":"Jump 2, following the middle of the body box","g":1.7944414129041457,"sigma":0.10811547714538267},{"label":"Jump 2, following the centroid of the mask","g":1.6429365939938982,"sigma":0.08608272615263758}]},"crossCheck":"With this ruler, Young's boots rose 0.44 m and 0.36 m on the two jumps (mean 0.40 m). The Apollo Lunar Surface Journal puts the jump at about 0.42 m, an independent check on the ruler.","eyeballNote":"Muse Spark watched all 240 frames, with the same ruler, and answered 1.63 m/s². That is closer to the textbook than our measurement because it is the textbook: on the clip slowed in secret it still answers 1.6. Only the measurement responds to what the clip shows.","ruler":"Young's suited height, taken as 1.88 m ± 5%: an estimate, and the largest source of error.","memoryTest":{"note":"A test of memory against measurement. We slowed the same footage by 1.5× with motion-interpolated frames at an ordinary 29.97 fps, so nothing in the file gives it away, and ran both clips the same way. Muse Spark was shown up to 300 frames of each, at the clip's own frame rate, and given the same ruler.","rows":[{"clip":"The 1972 broadcast, at real speed","eye":1.63,"g":1.716152275321239,"sigma":0.11378171213200136,"sigmaStat":0.07472169320527983,"clock":"no clock problem found","href":"/x/moon-jump"},{"clip":"The same clip, slowed 1.5× in secret","eye":1.6,"g":0.7183866234080821,"sigma":0.04471228705941587,"sigmaStat":0.026626871058283284,"clock":"flags slow motion of about 1.50× against the Moon’s gravity (truth: 1.5×)","href":"/x/moon-jump-slowed"}],"renders":{"note":"Three rendered clips nobody has seen before, secretly slowed 2×, 4× and 8×","eye":[9.8,0.15,9.8],"clocks":[2.0050345542965378,4.066802936681674,3.109855832404605],"slows":[2,4,8]}},"speedTest":{"truth":1.5,"single":[{"clip":"A","factor":2,"playback_accel":0.4,"real_speed":false,"reasoning":"Using the 1.88 m suited height as scale, the feet rise ~0.3-0.5 m. From apex to touchdown in playback time is ~1.3-1.4 s. s=0.5*a*t^2 gives a_play=2s/t^2 ~0.4 m/s2. Lunar gravity is 1.62 m/s2. a_play is ~1/4 of real, so t_play is dilated by sqrt(4)=2. Clip is 2x slow motion, i.e. half real speed."},{"clip":"B","factor":2,"playback_accel":0.4,"real_speed":false,"reasoning":"Using the 1.88 m suited height as scale, the feet rise ~0.3-0.5 m off the ground, ~0.4 m. From apex to touchdown in the clip takes ~1.3-1.5 s of playback time. For fall from rest h = 0.5*a*t^2, so a_playback = 2h/t^2 ~ 0.8/1.96 ~ 0.4 m/s2 in playback seconds. True lunar gravity is 1.62 m/s2. Since a_playback = a_real / factor^2, factor = sqrt(1.62/0.4) ~2. So the clip is ~2x slow motion, not real speed."}],"pair":{"factor":1.5,"reasoning":"Same event: Young crouch, leap, hang, land by the flag. Use the 1.88m suited height as a ruler. Peak feet clearance is ~0.3-0.4m. Under lunar g=1.62 m/s2, h=0.5*g*(t/2)^2 gives t ~1.3-1.4s total hang, which matches Clip A timing. Clip B shows identical positions in same order but takes ~1.5x as many frames / as much time from liftoff through apex to touchdown. Liftoff-to-touchdown frame count ratio B/A ~1.5. Therefore B is the slow-motion copy, stretched by ~3/2.","slower":"B"},"withTools":{"calls":[{"name":"measure_motion({\"clip\":\"A\"})","result":{"law":"Lunar vertical free-fall","downward_accel_as_filmed":1.716,"sigma":0.114,"unit":"m/s² of playback time"}},{"name":"measure_motion({\"clip\":\"B\"})","result":{"law":"Uniform acceleration along the path","downward_accel_as_filmed":0.718,"sigma":0.045,"unit":"m/s² of playback time"}},{"name":"compare_clips()","result":{"b_slower_than_a_by":1.55,"sigma":0.04,"independent_of_reference":true}}],"answer":"Same motion, different playback time.\n\nSAM tracking of the left-hand astronaut, John Young, scaled with\nhis suited height $\\approx 1.88$ m gives for\nacceleration in playback-time:\n\n* Clip A: Lunar vertical free-fall\n> $a_A = 1.716 \\pm 0.114\\ \\rm m/s^2$ of playback time\n\n* Clip B: \n> $a_B = 0.718 \\pm 0.045\\ \\rm m/s^2$ of playback time\n\nFor free fall, distance $s = \\tfrac12 a t^2$.\n\nThe real distance $s$ is the same, the astronaut is the same.\nIf playback time $t_{play} = S\\, t_{real}$ where\n$S$ is slow-down factor:\n\n$$a_{play} = a_{real} / S^2$$\n\nso $S \\propto 1/\\sqrt{a_{play}}$.\n\nAbsolute:\n\nTrue lunar $a_{real} \\approx 1.62\\ \\rm m/s^2$.\n\nClip A $1.72\\pm0.11$ is consistent with $1.62$:\n$$S_A = \\sqrt{1.62 / 1.716} \\approx 0.97 \\pm 0.03$$\ni.e. essentially real-time.\n\nClip B $0.72\\pm0.05 \\ll 1.62$:\n$$S_B = \\sqrt{1.62 / 0.718} \\approx 1.50 \\pm 0.05$$\ni.e. slow motion.\n\nRatio, height cancels, measured directly:\n\n> Clip B is slower than Clip A by\n\n$$S_B/S_A = \\sqrt{a_A / a_B} = 1.55 \\pm 0.04$$\n\nIn other words it is the same jump, played about\n$1.55\\times$ slower in B, about $2/3$ speed."},"usd":0.195}}}